course: dictionary
lesson: 4450
title: Hangover: a word with two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hangover is the sick feeling a person can have the day after drinking too much alcohol. «A hangover is how the body feels the day after drinking too much alcohol: headache, tiredness, thirst and feeling sick.» The list of feelings after the word shows you what it means.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Headache, thirst and feeling sick are all about being unwell, not about the weather or a meal. Swap in your meaning: «the sick feeling after drinking too much» fits the sentence, so you have it.
explain: Some words also carry an idea from the past. «A hangover from the old days» means something left over from an earlier time that still continues. The clue is «from the old days»: here the word is not about a sore head at all.
explain: Phrases give more clues. «Have a hangover» and «nurse a hangover» show that a hangover is something a person has and looks after. Alcohol is for grown-ups, so children mostly hear this word on TV, in the news or in stories about grown-ups.
fact: A hangover is the headache and sick feeling the day after drinking too much alcohol.
fact: «Hair of the dog» means an alcoholic drink taken to cure a hangover, but it does not really work.
fact: «Worse for wear» means drunk or badly hungover, in Britain.
fact: A hangover can also mean something left over from an earlier time that still continues, such as «a hangover from the old days».
fact: Phrases with hangover include «have a hangover» and «nurse a hangover».
exercise: «A hangover is how the body feels the day after drinking too much alcohol: headache, tiredness, thirst and feeling sick.» What is a hangover? | A very loud noise at night | A gift left over from a party | A type of hot drink | *A sick feeling, headache and tiredness the day after drinking too much alcohol
exercise: Mia reads «a hangover from the old days» in a history book. What does hangover mean here? | A party for the whole village | A long walk home | *Something left over from an earlier time that still continues | The sick feeling after drinking too much
exercise: What does «hair of the dog» mean? | *An alcoholic drink taken to try to cure a hangover | A pet dog that has been brushed | A story about a dog | A hairstyle for a sunny day
test: «Hair of the dog» is said to cure a hangover, but what does the dictionary say about it? | It makes you taller | It always works | *It does not really work | It is a healthy drink
test: In «nurse a hangover», what does a hangover seem to be? | A sort of cake | A type of train | A school subject | *Something a person has and looks after
exam: Which sentence uses hangover to mean something left over from an earlier time? | *The old rule is a hangover from the days before cars | He nursed a cup of tea | They went swimming | She had a hangover after the party
